WEBVTT

00:00:00.322 --> 00:00:02.517
Last video ended on a stack of chairs.

00:00:03.422 --> 00:00:05.927
One chair is eighty centimetres tall, and every

00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:08.453
chair you add makes the stack six centimetres

00:00:08.494 --> 00:00:08.854
taller.

00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:11.670
So how tall is a stack of twenty

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:11.992
chairs?

00:00:13.061 --> 00:00:13.664
Here's a quick guess.

00:00:14.471 --> 00:00:17.086
Start at eighty, and add twenty sixes.

00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:20.101
That gives two hundred centimetres, so it would

00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:22.572
only just fit under a two-metre door.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.976
It sounds right, but is it?

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:27.084
By the end of the next few minutes,

00:00:27.446 --> 00:00:29.553
you'll know how to find any term, and

00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:30.858
whether that guess survives.

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:35.331
A sequence like eighty, eighty-six, ninety-two is called

00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:38.941
an arithmetic sequence, or an arithmetic progression, AP

00:00:38.961 --> 00:00:39.342
for short.

00:00:40.004 --> 00:00:42.489
It's arithmetic when every difference between a term

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.373
and the term before it is the same.

00:00:45.056 --> 00:00:47.664
That fixed difference is called the common difference,

00:00:47.966 --> 00:00:48.166
d.

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:52.494
Here, eighty-six minus eighty is six, ninety-two minus

00:00:52.535 --> 00:00:55.139
eighty-six is six, and so on, so d

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:55.619
is six.

00:00:56.443 --> 00:00:57.729
The difference can be negative, too.

00:00:58.272 --> 00:01:02.378
Twenty, seventeen, fourteen, eleven goes down by three

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.803
each step, so d is minus three.

00:01:05.585 --> 00:01:08.893
But one, four, nine, sixteen isn't arithmetic.

00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:12.183
Its differences are three, five and seven, which

00:01:12.324 --> 00:01:12.824
aren't the same.

00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.492
So to test a list of terms, check

00:01:15.513 --> 00:01:16.756
that each difference matches.

00:01:17.499 --> 00:01:19.368
For a sequence given by a rule, show

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:21.357
the difference is the same for every n.

00:01:22.022 --> 00:01:24.316
So how do we jump straight to any

00:01:24.377 --> 00:01:24.658
term?

00:01:25.342 --> 00:01:27.890
Start at the first term, a, one chair.

00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:30.971
To reach the second term, add one d.

00:01:31.616 --> 00:01:34.147
The third term is two d's along, and

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.176
the fourth term is three d's along.

00:01:36.839 --> 00:01:38.966
Each time, the number of steps is one

00:01:39.086 --> 00:01:40.350
less than the position,

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.698
because the first chair needed no step at

00:01:42.798 --> 00:01:42.918
all.

00:01:43.521 --> 00:01:46.595
So the nth term is a, plus n

00:01:46.656 --> 00:01:48.202
minus one lots of d.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:49.857
Now test the guess.

00:01:50.564 --> 00:01:53.291
A stack of twenty chairs is nineteen steps

00:01:53.453 --> 00:01:54.495
above one chair.

00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:58.222
That's eighty plus nineteen sixes, which is one

00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:01.007
hundred and ninety-four centimetres, not two hundred.

00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:03.518
The guess added one chair too many.

00:02:04.155 --> 00:02:07.085
Here's why arithmetic sequences look so familiar.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.021
Plot each stack's height against the number of

00:02:10.102 --> 00:02:10.443
chairs.

00:02:11.248 --> 00:02:13.591
Every extra chair goes up by d, the

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.976
same amount each time, so the points lie

00:02:16.078 --> 00:02:16.878
on a straight line.

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:19.846
Expand the formula, and you get d n,

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:21.470
plus a minus d.

00:02:22.273 --> 00:02:24.158
That's a linear function of n, and its

00:02:24.199 --> 00:02:25.141
gradient is d.

00:02:25.964 --> 00:02:28.429
For the chairs, a sub n is six

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.295
n plus seventy-four, so the line has gradient

00:02:31.356 --> 00:02:31.655
six.

00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:34.805
It would cross the vertical axis at seventy-four,

00:02:35.187 --> 00:02:37.795
but there's no stack of zero chairs, because

00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:38.778
n starts at one.

00:02:39.521 --> 00:02:41.645
And the points sit only at whole numbers,

00:02:41.987 --> 00:02:43.749
because you can't stack part of a chair,

00:02:44.131 --> 00:02:45.212
so we don't join them up.

00:02:45.783 --> 00:02:46.616
Let's use the formula.

00:02:47.402 --> 00:02:50.569
An arithmetic sequence has first term five, and

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:51.772
common difference three.

00:02:52.374 --> 00:02:53.688
Find the tenth term.

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:56.458
That's nine steps of three after the first

00:02:56.498 --> 00:02:59.785
term, so a sub ten is five plus

00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:01.388
twenty-seven, which is thirty-two.

00:03:02.271 --> 00:03:03.798
Now, which term equals fifty?

00:03:04.585 --> 00:03:06.196
Set the formula equal to fifty.

00:03:06.861 --> 00:03:08.868
Five, plus n minus one lots of three,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:09.611
equals fifty.

00:03:10.274 --> 00:03:11.095
Take away five,

00:03:11.598 --> 00:03:13.181
and n minus one lots of three is

00:03:13.222 --> 00:03:13.883
forty-five.

00:03:14.385 --> 00:03:16.580
Divide by three, and n minus one is

00:03:16.641 --> 00:03:17.063
fifteen.

00:03:17.547 --> 00:03:20.181
So n is sixteen, and fifty is the

00:03:20.282 --> 00:03:21.066
sixteenth term.

00:03:21.769 --> 00:03:22.009
Check:

00:03:22.572 --> 00:03:24.638
five plus fifteen threes is fifty.

00:03:25.424 --> 00:03:26.374
Here's a useful twist.

00:03:27.041 --> 00:03:29.335
Can a stack be exactly one hundred and

00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:30.342
fifty centimetres tall?

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:33.001
Set the formula equal to one hundred and

00:03:33.041 --> 00:03:33.323
fifty.

00:03:34.107 --> 00:03:36.253
Eighty, plus n minus one lots of six,

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:37.937
equals one hundred and fifty.

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:41.184
So n minus one is seventy over six,

00:03:41.625 --> 00:03:43.810
and n is twelve and two thirds.

00:03:44.553 --> 00:03:46.322
That's not a whole number, and you can't

00:03:46.363 --> 00:03:47.670
have two thirds of a chair.

00:03:48.455 --> 00:03:50.301
So one hundred and fifty is not a

00:03:50.342 --> 00:03:50.602
term.

00:03:51.486 --> 00:03:53.469
On the graph, the line passes one hundred

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.873
and fifty between the twelfth and thirteenth points,

00:03:56.215 --> 00:03:58.338
which are one hundred and forty-six and one

00:03:58.359 --> 00:03:59.099
hundred and fifty-two.

00:03:59.701 --> 00:04:01.749
Sometimes you're given two terms instead.

00:04:02.511 --> 00:04:04.518
The fourth term is thirteen, and the ninth

00:04:04.558 --> 00:04:05.682
term is thirty-three.

00:04:06.284 --> 00:04:07.311
Find a and d.

00:04:08.255 --> 00:04:10.098
From the fourth term to the ninth is

00:04:10.219 --> 00:04:10.820
five steps,

00:04:11.421 --> 00:04:14.246
and the value rises by twenty, from thirteen

00:04:14.306 --> 00:04:14.887
to thirty-three.

00:04:15.651 --> 00:04:18.306
So five d is twenty, and d is

00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:18.688
four.

00:04:19.354 --> 00:04:21.227
That's the same as subtracting the two equations.

00:04:22.054 --> 00:04:24.268
Now step back from the fourth term to

00:04:24.289 --> 00:04:24.751
the first.

00:04:25.416 --> 00:04:28.466
That's three steps of four, so a is

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.935
thirteen minus twelve, which is one.

00:04:31.699 --> 00:04:31.959
Check:

00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:35.213
the ninth term is one plus eight fours,

00:04:35.616 --> 00:04:36.358
which is thirty-three.

00:04:37.098 --> 00:04:38.631
Now a decreasing one.

00:04:39.216 --> 00:04:42.268
An arithmetic sequence starts at seven, with common

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:43.653
difference minus four.

00:04:44.397 --> 00:04:46.527
Which term is the first negative one?

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.736
The formula gives a sub n equals seven,

00:04:50.218 --> 00:04:53.124
plus n minus one lots of minus four,

00:04:53.506 --> 00:04:56.052
which simplifies to eleven minus four n.

00:04:56.856 --> 00:04:58.141
Set that below zero.

00:04:58.745 --> 00:05:01.134
Then four n is bigger than eleven, so

00:05:01.516 --> 00:05:03.844
n is bigger than two point seven five.

00:05:04.609 --> 00:05:06.419
But n is a position, so it must

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.305
be a whole number.

00:05:07.829 --> 00:05:09.579
The first whole number above two point seven

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.827
five is three.

00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:13.255
Check: the terms are seven,

00:05:13.757 --> 00:05:15.280
three, and then minus one.

00:05:15.823 --> 00:05:17.889
And the gradient here is minus four, the

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:18.471
same as d.

00:05:19.276 --> 00:05:21.970
To remember why it's n minus one, think

00:05:22.011 --> 00:05:22.090
of

00:05:22.654 --> 00:05:23.297
fence posts.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.948
Five posts in a row have only four

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:26.811
gaps between them.

00:05:27.435 --> 00:05:29.442
Terms are the posts, and the steps are

00:05:29.463 --> 00:05:29.844
the gaps.

00:05:30.487 --> 00:05:33.550
So the nth term sits n minus one

00:05:33.631 --> 00:05:34.972
steps after the first.

00:05:37.762 --> 00:05:38.145
Your turn.

00:05:39.011 --> 00:05:42.419
An arithmetic sequence has first term twelve, and

00:05:42.499 --> 00:05:44.083
common difference minus five.

00:05:44.648 --> 00:05:46.063
Find the twentieth term.

00:05:46.649 --> 00:05:47.476
Pause here to answer.

00:05:58.201 --> 00:06:01.140
The twentieth term is nineteen steps after the

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:01.482
first.

00:06:02.044 --> 00:06:04.968
So it's twelve, plus nineteen lots of minus

00:06:05.008 --> 00:06:05.309
five.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.642
That's twelve minus ninety-five, which is minus eighty-three.

00:06:10.589 --> 00:06:12.025
Minus eighty-eight is wrong.

00:06:12.428 --> 00:06:14.357
It takes twenty steps, which is one too

00:06:14.398 --> 00:06:14.599
many.

00:06:16.257 --> 00:06:16.761
Last one.

00:06:17.446 --> 00:06:19.833
The third term of an arithmetic sequence is

00:06:19.954 --> 00:06:20.315
ten,

00:06:20.837 --> 00:06:22.602
and the seventh term is twenty-six.

00:06:23.345 --> 00:06:25.949
Find the first term and the common difference.

00:06:26.737 --> 00:06:27.566
Pause here to answer.

00:06:38.435 --> 00:06:40.402
From the third term to the seventh is

00:06:40.544 --> 00:06:41.186
four steps,

00:06:41.809 --> 00:06:43.576
and the value rises by sixteen.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.135
So d is four.

00:06:46.021 --> 00:06:48.402
Now step back two places from the third

00:06:48.443 --> 00:06:48.723
term:

00:06:49.404 --> 00:06:50.824
ten minus eight is two,

00:06:51.326 --> 00:06:52.326
so a is two.

00:06:53.168 --> 00:06:54.917
Three point two is wrong for d.

00:06:55.561 --> 00:06:58.329
It divides by five, counting the terms instead

00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.392
of the steps between them.

00:07:00.954 --> 00:07:04.316
So a fixed difference d means every term

00:07:04.437 --> 00:07:06.677
is a, plus n minus one lots of

00:07:06.738 --> 00:07:06.957
d,

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:09.279
and the terms lie on a straight line

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:09.800
in n.

00:07:10.723 --> 00:07:11.889
Next, the total.

00:07:12.513 --> 00:07:14.697
A supermarket stacks tins in a pyramid:

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:17.483
one on top, two below it, then three,

00:07:17.845 --> 00:07:19.608
down to fifteen on the bottom row.

00:07:20.251 --> 00:07:21.416
How many tins is that?

00:07:22.242 --> 00:07:23.754
Adding them one row at a time is

00:07:23.836 --> 00:07:24.178
slow.

00:07:24.804 --> 00:07:25.630
Is there a shortcut?

00:07:28.388 --> 00:07:29.271
Here are the key notes.

00:07:29.794 --> 00:07:31.562
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:31.583 --> 00:07:32.064
to copy them.
